00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.450
There's still a lot to learn.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.840
If you take two
random people, you'll

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.720
find that they
differ in about 0.1%

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.950
of their genome, of their DNA.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.030
So that's about three
million letters.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:21.310
And DNA is particularly
interesting,

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.910
because one of the reasons is
that the cost has gone down

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:25.850
a lot.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:27.380
You may have seen
this graph before

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.597
if you've gone to any talk about
sequencing DNA or reading DNA.

00:01:31.597 --> 00:01:32.930
But we'll go through it quickly.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.030
So the x-axis is time.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.400
The y-axis is cost.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.550
This is a logarithmic scale.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.680
The green line is the
cost of reading the DNA.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.450
And as you can see,
it has crashed.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.002
The comparison line in
white is Moore's Law,

00:01:47.002 --> 00:01:48.460
so it has-- the
price has gone down

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.890
even faster than Moore's Law.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.380
And so now, it
costs roughly $1,000

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.730
to sequence somebody's DNA.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.340
So this is fantastic.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.000
And this means that we do
it a lot more than before,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.680
because now it's cheaper.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.190
Reading DNA is really nice
for a number of reasons.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:10.060
It's not just about
reading the genome,

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.850
and understanding the
ancestry, and figuring out

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.400
the ways in which
people are different.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.000
You could also sequence
somebody's cancer tumor

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.010
to try to find which
medicine are going to work.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.520
You can also do other
things than reading DNA,

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.410
using the exact same technology.

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.990
So one of the things
that people do

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.680
is they read the RNA that
is active in the cells,

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.740
as they're creating proteins.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:40.060
So what this does is that,
whereas the DNA is mostly

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.430
static if you're healthy,
so you get your DNA

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:43.660
and then that's what it is.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.100
It determines the
blueprints of your body.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.980
Reading the RNA tells you
what this particular cell is

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.550
doing at this particular time.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.051
So this is something that you
can explore different tissues,

00:02:55.051 --> 00:02:56.883
different places in
your body that are going

00:02:56.883 --> 00:02:58.270
to be doing different things.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.640
And then at different
times, it may

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.090
be doing different things,
perhaps healthy versus sick.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.090
And you do this using the
exact same technology.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.730
So the price, the
crashing of price,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:13.120
means that we can now do this
RNA reading also very cheaply.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.940
Another thing that
people do is you can,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.700
because this instrument
works so well,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.230
we use it to measure
other things.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.570
So we use DNA sequencing
to find proteins.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.530
So the way this works,
just briefly, is we can--

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.210
and by we I mean the field--

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.140
we can engineer and,
hopefully I get this right.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.640
I'm a computer scientist, right?

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.920
So we can engineer an
antibody that will attach

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.140
to this particular protein.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.480
And we can attach
a bit of DNA to it.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.880
So what we do is we just
mix this in the blood,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.540
or whatever we
are interested in.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.060
And then we sequence this.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.114
We wash it.

00:03:49.114 --> 00:03:51.530
We wash the remaining liquid
off, and then we sequence it.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.420
And so if that particular
protein was there,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.822
then the antibody
attached to it.

00:03:55.822 --> 00:03:58.030
And that we will find the
DNA that we're looking for.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:03:59.970
So we can-- you can
read DNA in order

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:03.480
to find proteins or things
that are completely,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.280
that are not directly
related to people's DNA.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:06.780
OK.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.080
So this is why I'm very
excited about reading DNA.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.150
The point I wanted
to make here also

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:17.700
is that the cost of doing the
lab part has gone down a lot.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.700
And in comparison,
the cost of doing

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.410
the computer part,
the analysis part,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.220
has not gone down as much.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.070
So in relative terms,
the computation

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.344
is now more important.

00:04:27.344 --> 00:04:29.760
And so you want to spend a
little bit of effort trying to,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.550
perhaps, make it go faster,
or cheaper, depending

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.556
on what you're optimizing for.

00:04:35.556 --> 00:04:36.930
And this is
amplified by the fact

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.011
that we now have even more
data than before, because it's

00:04:40.011 --> 00:04:41.010
cheap, so we do it more.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.520
So we want to do-- so
computation is relatively more

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.390
expensive.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.720
And there's also more of it.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.960
So we'll be talking
about some computation.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.080
So now, let me tell
you a little bit

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.640
about how reading
DNA works, just

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.650
so I can motivate the problem
that we're trying to solve.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.471
So a typical lab would receive
perhaps 100,000 samples a year.

00:05:00.471 --> 00:05:02.470
And the sample would be
perhaps a vial of blood.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:03.815
It could be other things.

00:05:03.815 --> 00:05:05.190
And the first
thing you do is you

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:10.530
try to extract
the DNA from this,

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.650
and amplify it, get a
higher concentration of DNA.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:17.100
Perhaps you'll have 10 times
as much as you started from.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.130
And then, we cut it
into tiny fragments.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.140
And this is because the machines
can only read short bits of DNA

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:23.910
at all.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.410
So we need to cut
it, so the machine

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.214
can access these parts--

00:05:27.214 --> 00:05:29.130
a lot of fragments really,
perhaps 100 million

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.200
fragments in this example.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.390
And so DNA, by the
way, has a direction,

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.900
so we don't worry too much
about it flipping around.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.416
And then you put
it in the machine.

00:05:38.416 --> 00:05:39.540
This is an example machine.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.490
It doesn't have to be this one.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.680
The machine is going to
read these fragments.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.180
And there, some
things can go wrong.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.340
These machines are not perfect.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.750
So one of the things that
can go wrong is duplicates.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:56.100
So bits of DNA can
show up in the output

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.320
as if they were there twice
as much as they really

00:05:58.320 --> 00:05:59.874
are in the sample.

00:05:59.874 --> 00:06:01.290
And this is the
problem that we're

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.460
going to work on in this talk.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.540
So one of the reasons
this can happen

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:10.450
is there is an optical
part in this process.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:13.010
So the picture here
symbolizes a bit of DNA.

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:15.300
And on the top are
the reads, so you

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.170
recognize these are the letters
I told you comprise DNA.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.600
And if the thing is a
little bit out of focus,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.210
it's possible that instead
of seeing one dot of light,

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.100
it thinks there are two dots.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.800
And so that would mean
that each one of these

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.740
now shows up twice
in the output.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:37.900
And this can happen, you know
as much as for 25% of the reads.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.310
So it's significant.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.360
And it's a problem.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.560
Because when you
do the analysis,

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.250
you really care about
the relative ratio

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.620
of each particular bit of DNA.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.920
And so if these ratios are out
of whack because of errors,

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:54.620
your analysis may be wrong,
or slightly off target.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.060
So we want to detect these
duplicates and remove them,

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.490
so that we can restore the
independence assumption that

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:02.380
underlies some of
the analysis that you

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:04.760
want to run downstream.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.660
Now, for the context here is
how the rest of the bits happen.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.710
So the first image
here is in the machine.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:15.830
So remember, we cut the DNA
into these small fragments.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:17.560
Then, the machine
is going to read

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:21.370
from each end of the
fragment, going inwards.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:26.230
And it can only read
roughly 100 bases down.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:27.690
And so they won't
actually make--

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:28.690
they won't meet in the middle.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:29.710
They will just
read a little bit,

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:31.084
and then the
machine has to stop.

00:07:31.084 --> 00:07:34.270
So you have these
two reads, that we

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.580
know they're close to
each other in the genome.

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.130
And so we say that
they're a pair.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:41.460
They go together.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.130
And, of course,
then we flip them

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:44.840
so they are at the right spot.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.910
So the green box onward is what
happens outside of the machine.

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.150
This is now part of
the computer stuff.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:53.390
And so the first thing we do
is we flip them right side up.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:55.185
And then, based on the
letters that we see,

00:07:55.185 --> 00:07:57.560
we are going to-- there's of
course more letters than are

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.690
just shown on the slide.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:03.080
We're going to compare this
to the reference genome,

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.120
and figure out
where in the genome

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.860
this particular read should be.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:11.354
So this read's represented by
the number here on the left.

00:08:11.354 --> 00:08:12.520
And you see the dotted line.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.990
So the first one
starts at position 100.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.230
Of course, we'd also say
which chromosome it's at.

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:19.150
Here, I'm simplifying
for the talk.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:20.800
So you have the
position for each.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.960
And then, because we
multiplied, we amplify the DNA

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.810
at the first stage, you don't
have just one strand of DNA

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:26.560
in there.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.710
So it's possible
that they overlap.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.210
And so you can use this
overlap to perhaps find errors.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.159
So one of the errors that the
machine can do-- we already

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:34.210
talked about duplication.

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:35.840
Another error they
can make is just

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.750
misread one of the letters.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.030
So perhaps you have two reads
like the two on the left

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:42.370
here in the red
box that overlap.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:45.020
And then you would expect
the letters to be the same.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.560
And in this case, there is one
place where they're different.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:52.635
So this could be because
it's one from the father, one

00:08:52.635 --> 00:08:53.260
for the mother.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.301
Or it could be because
there was a reading error.

00:08:55.301 --> 00:08:59.020
And so if you have multiple
reads that overlap,

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:00.700
and you have removed
the duplicates,

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:02.866
then you can perhaps use
majority rule, or something

00:09:02.866 --> 00:09:05.440
like this, to figure out
what's the reality there

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.610
and which part was an error.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.420
So this is an example where
removing the duplicates

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:11.830
matters.

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:14.710
And yes so a file like this
may be 50 gigabytes or so,

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.720
just to give you an
idea of the scale.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:23.300
So what this looks like in
the computer, on the top

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:24.890
I have the symbolic
representation.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:27.470
You have the helix of the DNA.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.590
And you have the
reads that we found.

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.640
So on the computer,
from our point of view

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:36.950
here today it's just a record
with these four fields.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:39.680
And importantly,
if two reads come

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.140
from the same fragment
of DNA, so they're both

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.870
read from different ends of
the same fragment of DNA,

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.079
we say that they're paired.

00:09:46.079 --> 00:09:47.870
And we're going to give
them the same name.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:49.880
This is how we know
they go together.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.350
And there is a paired flag
that we're going to set to yes,

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:55.190
so that we remember
that these go together.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.800
I represent them
with warmer colors

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.036
for the reads that are
paired and a cooler color

00:10:00.036 --> 00:10:01.160
for the reads that are not.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.660
But you don't need
to remember this.

00:10:05.481 --> 00:10:05.980
All right.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.190
So here's the takeaway
from the biology.

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:10.647
So you don't need to
remember the biology.

00:10:10.647 --> 00:10:12.730
But all that really matters
from the point of view

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:14.438
of the computation
we'll be talking about

00:10:14.438 --> 00:10:16.690
is that the data
structure we work with

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.460
is a set of read records.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.440
And these read records
have the fields that

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.810
are here on the screen.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:26.860
Importantly, they have a name,
a pair flag and a position.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.050
And if two reads
have the same name,

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:30.460
that means that they are paired.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.420
And their paired file is
going to be set to yes.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.580
The task is to set the flag
that's circled here called

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:39.487
is duplicate, because we're
looking for the duplicates.

00:10:39.487 --> 00:10:41.070
I will tell you in
the next slide what

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.572
are the rules for doing this.

00:10:42.572 --> 00:10:44.030
And the output is
of the same form.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:45.405
It's a set of
records, but now we

00:10:45.405 --> 00:10:48.530
filled in this form, this field.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:49.030
All right.

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:51.682
So the rules, now this slide
looks a little bit busy,

00:10:51.682 --> 00:10:52.390
but bear with me.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:54.200
This is actually fairly simple.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:56.090
We're comparing reads.

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:58.050
Now, they can be paired or not.

00:10:58.050 --> 00:10:59.580
So there's three cases.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:03.350
Either they're both unpaired,
or both paired, or one of each.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:05.700
We'll do each case at a time.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.460
They're just the different rows.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:09.270
So the first case is
unpaired, so suppose

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:10.800
you have two unpaired reads.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.230
The rule that we use
is simply, if they

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:15.506
both have the same
position, then

00:11:15.506 --> 00:11:16.630
we say they are duplicates.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:19.170
And we're going to
mark one of them.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:22.170
And I represent this in the
example in the middle column.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.440
You can see the green
and blue reads here.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.690
I aligned them to the
same horizontal position.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:30.330
I'm using this to symbolically
represent the fact that they

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.370
have the same position
in the genome,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:35.980
so their position field
has the same value.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:37.050
So they're duplicates.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:39.810
And so I circle one that we're
going to mark as duplicate.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:42.810
The column on the right shows
an example of a non-duplicate.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:43.500
They're offset.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.340
They don't have
the same position.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:46.740
So we don't mark them.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.980
Now for pairs, the
middle row, the rule

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:52.240
is that both
positions must match.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.630
So if you have paired reads,
if you have two pairs,

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:56.822
then the position of
each element of the pair

00:11:56.822 --> 00:11:58.530
must match with the
corresponding element

00:11:58.530 --> 00:11:59.832
from the other pair.

00:11:59.832 --> 00:12:01.290
So you can see in
the example here,

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:05.070
I have a green pair
and a orange pair.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:08.890
And they must align on
the left and on the right.

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:11.610
On the other, the
counter-example on the right,

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:14.250
you can see if any one
of them is offset then

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.350
we don't mark anything.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:17.910
The final case is mixed.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.330
If you have one paired
read and one unpaired read,

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:22.830
then it's only the unpaired
read that's going to get marked.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.800
And it's going to be marked if
it's position matches either

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:29.430
of the elements of the pair.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.424
So it's always kind
of the same rule.

00:12:31.424 --> 00:12:33.090
It's just, if there's
only one position,

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:35.010
it's the position must match.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.450
You have two positions,
both positions must match.

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:41.400
And if it's a mix, one of
the positions must match.

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:45.340
So that's the rule that
we have to implement.

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:50.060
And now, before we start
talking about tool,

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:54.080
I want to give you just a
quick overview of the landscape

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:55.820
of things that you could use.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:57.350
This is very high
level, and it's

00:12:57.350 --> 00:12:59.510
kind of my own way
of thinking of it.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.990
So the way I think of it is
on the one hand on the left,

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:05.700
you have what I'm calling
servers as a service.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:10.640
This is where you use the
cloud just to rent the computer

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:13.300
and run your thing on it.

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.920
And so you can take an existing
program and just put it there.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:17.060
And it'll work just fine.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:21.200
This is the easiest thing to
do because it's very familiar.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.490
And so this is great.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.360
For example, if you already have
some existing legacy already

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:29.660
written software, you can
just run it there as is.

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:33.050
You would perhaps use
a tool like Kubernetes.

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.750
In life science, we have
developed our own tools.

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:37.590
There is Cromwell.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:40.492
At Verily, we put together
a dsub, which is an--

00:13:40.492 --> 00:13:41.640
it's for our users.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:44.600
It's a simple tool for
running things on the cloud,

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:46.320
on the server.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.590
Or you may want to go
a little bit further

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:50.240
down the abstraction
route and use

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:52.160
what I'm calling a
database-centric approach,

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:55.400
where you use a database
abstraction that the cloud can

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.640
give you, such as BigQuery.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:02.032
And so here, this is great when
your data is mostly static.

00:14:02.032 --> 00:14:03.740
Perhaps you've already
done the analysis,

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:05.974
now you just want to
present it in a fancy way.

00:14:05.974 --> 00:14:08.390
And you want to be able to
have multiple computers serving

00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:12.530
the data, so lots of people can
go and look at your results.

00:14:12.530 --> 00:14:15.560
And the one on the right
is pushing the abstraction

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:16.970
a little bit further.

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:19.761
Now, that's the
data flow languages.

00:14:19.761 --> 00:14:21.260
So the data flow
languages allow you

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.220
to get a lot of parallelism.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:26.900
And the idea here
is that we're now

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:28.452
representing your
computation-- this

00:14:28.452 --> 00:14:30.410
is for your computation--
we're representing it

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:33.830
as a graph, where the nodes
represent things that happen

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:35.780
to the data, so computation.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:38.330
And the edges represent
the data flowing,

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:40.620
that's why we call them
a data flow language.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.164
So in this example
picture here, we

00:14:42.164 --> 00:14:44.330
have three different things
that happen to the data.

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:46.830
And the data flows
from one to the other.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:49.409
And there are multiple
such languages.

00:14:49.409 --> 00:14:51.200
There's Apache Beam,
that we'll talk about.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:53.300
You may have heard
of Spark also.

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:55.594
So you get parallelism,
because each

00:14:55.594 --> 00:14:57.260
of these three steps
here in the picture

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:58.620
can be done at the same time.

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:00.590
Each of these are maybe
different computers

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.090
running at the same time.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:04.100
And also each
individual step can

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:09.330
be done in cooperative
manner, by multiple computers.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:12.970
So let's give you the
punchline of the talk already.

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:18.770
So we started from a
non-optimized single server

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:19.640
running this thing.

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.670
And then we wanted to
write it with Beam.

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:23.390
And we did.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:27.530
And the result is five times
faster at only twice the cost.

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:29.540
So we're pretty happy
with the result.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:31.160
But let's see how we did this.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.800
Why did we choose Apache Beam?

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:36.950
Apache Beam is good
when you're working

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:41.100
on a large set of elements
that are all of the same type.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.130
This is the case here
with the genomics read.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:45.410
We have a large number of them.

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:47.570
We can process them in parallel.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:50.120
This is a common
pattern in life science.

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:53.690
And you may recognize the same
pattern in your own field.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:57.050
You know, it's not uncommon to
have many receipts, or images,

00:15:57.050 --> 00:16:00.500
or purchases, or
clicks, or views,

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:04.310
or ad impressions,
things like that.

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:08.040
The best way I know to explain
Beam is through an example.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:09.930
So we'll just jump right in.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.020
So suppose what you're
trying to do is--

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:15.230
so still in the genomic context
that we're talking about--

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:19.190
suppose we want to know, for
each position in the genome,

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:22.440
how many reads do we have
at that particular position?

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:24.800
So in the example here,
we go from left to right.

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:27.610
On the left we have storage
with our set of reads.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.570
There are two blue
reads at position 12

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:32.290
and a red read at position 20.

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:35.000
So the way you do
this with Beam is you

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:36.800
would start by
grouping by position.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:40.730
So group by would
put elements together

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.315
that match the thing
that you specify.

00:16:42.315 --> 00:16:43.940
So position in this
case, so you end up

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:46.490
with two groups, a
group at position 12,

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.230
a group at position 20.

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.240
And the next step you
would do is you would say,

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:53.010
now I want to count how many
elements are in each group.

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:54.470
And so you get your answer.

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:56.030
At position 12,
I have two reads.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:57.830
At position 20, I have one.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:16:58.847
We're done.

00:16:58.847 --> 00:16:59.930
That's what it looks like.

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.190
It's a very simple
example, of course.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.420
But at least I didn't
give you word count.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:08.150
Now, this is the
abstraction, right?

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.760
So what happens when
you run this in Beam

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:13.760
is that your code
remains simple.

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:15.160
So your code is--

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:16.670
I put pseudocode
here on the left.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.560
It matches these
simple concepts.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:23.390
But the runtime can actually
leverage the simple description

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:24.510
to give you parallelism.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:27.800
So you saw where, whereas
before we had one yellow

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:30.860
dot representing
one computation.

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.290
When you hit Run, it ends
up being multiple machines.

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:36.530
I put two on the
slide for space.

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:38.882
So what can happen
is the compiler

00:17:38.882 --> 00:17:40.340
recognizes that
you're trying to do

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:42.050
an operation on every element.

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:43.290
This can be done in parallel.

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:44.660
So let's do it.

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:47.780
It recognizes ah, you're reading
these files from the cloud.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:50.300
Well, it so happens that
the cloud replicates files

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:51.950
for availabilities,
so we can actually

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:54.920
read from different replicas
to get more performance.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:58.910
And it will do all of these
things for you transparently.

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:00.830
One cool thing it can
do also is actually

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:04.160
it can watch your
computation as it goes,

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.790
and potentially add or remove
computers to tune things

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:11.000
as it's going.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:15.380
So I wanted to say, in case
you're familiar with MapReduce,

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:17.750
this may look very
familiar to you.

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:20.280
And so indeed, Apache
Beam is actually,

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:22.320
it's a generalization
of MapReduce.

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:24.230
So in the sample example,
it looks the same.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:28.550
But Beam differs in,
I would say, two ways.

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:30.380
The first is that
instead of just being

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:34.250
one map and one reduce, you can
do an arbitrary number of these

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:36.472
together in the same
program, without having

00:18:36.472 --> 00:18:37.430
to do anything special.

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:39.560
So it doesn't have
to be two steps.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:42.230
And the other difference is it
has more operations than just

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:43.070
these two.

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:46.400
So let's go through some of the
operations that Beam can do.

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:48.410
So here is an
example Beam program.

00:18:48.410 --> 00:18:49.040
It's a graph.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:50.307
They all look like graphs.

00:18:50.307 --> 00:18:51.890
This program is
completely artificial.

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:53.723
It's just there to show
the different things

00:18:53.723 --> 00:18:55.290
that Beam can do.

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:59.480
So the first computation
here, marked A,

00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:01.130
I wanted to show
that Beam can have

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:03.830
multiple inputs coming
into the same computation,

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:04.910
and multiple outputs.

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:07.680
So you're not limited
to simple graphs.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:11.010
And when you send
to multiple places,

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:13.742
it could be you send the
same data to everybody.

00:19:13.742 --> 00:19:15.200
You have different
sub-computations

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:16.783
that you need to do
in the same input.

00:19:16.783 --> 00:19:19.380
Or perhaps, you send
different subsets of your data

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:20.630
to different sub-computations.

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.060
Perhaps, I don't know,
all the green images

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:24.500
do some-- get some processing.

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:27.870
And all the blue images
processed in a different way.

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:30.970
So that's multiple
inputs and outputs.

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:35.440
The next dot, the B circle
here shows the map operation.

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:38.360
You apply an arbitrary
function to each element.

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:40.760
So in the picture here,
I'm just bolding them.

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:43.660
Of course, you can write
arbitrary code, you

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:46.580
just, whatever code you like
to process the elements.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:48.682
And when you put
this in Beam, you

00:19:48.682 --> 00:19:50.390
will know to put it
on multiple computers

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:53.480
and apply this to all the
elements in your collection.

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:56.610
You can also add or remove
elements from a collection.

00:19:56.610 --> 00:19:58.340
So this is the next dot here.

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:02.660
You see I've added the Pac-Man
shape and removed another.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:08.906
The next one, D, is combining.

00:20:08.906 --> 00:20:10.280
Here, I'm showing
just the count.

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:11.600
So we have three shapes.

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.260
And I get the count.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.230
There's a number of
built-in combiners.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:16.170
You can do a max.

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:19.130
You can do a sum,
minimum obviously.

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.620
But the neat thing is actually
you can write your own.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.130
So any piece of
code, so long as it's

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:26.665
associative and
commutative, the Beam

00:20:26.665 --> 00:20:28.580
will know how to
run it efficiently

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:31.520
on multiple machines, and run
a little computation tree,

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:34.220
aggregation tree, and get you
the right answer efficiently.

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:36.350
And you don't need to do
anything, you just specify

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:39.670
the aggregation function.

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:41.820
The final one on this slide,
there's actually more,

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:44.964
but the final one here we're
talking about is group by.

00:20:44.964 --> 00:20:46.380
You've seen group
by before, where

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.390
we're grouping by a position.

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:50.610
So it takes a set of
elements and it gives you

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:54.030
a set of groups of elements.

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:57.601
Group by in Beam actually has
another really nice feature

00:20:57.601 --> 00:20:59.850
that I just want to mention
now, even though we're not

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:04.600
using here, is that Apache Beam
can run in a streaming mode.

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:08.257
So instead, whereas in a
normal, usual computation

00:21:08.257 --> 00:21:09.090
you have some input.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:10.381
And you run it on your program.

00:21:10.381 --> 00:21:12.086
And you get an output,
and you're done.

00:21:12.086 --> 00:21:13.710
In a streaming
computation, the program

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:16.230
just keeps on running
forever, conceptually.

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:18.990
And you have inputs
coming in as streaming in,

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:22.300
perhaps they're publish
subscribes, events, or clicks,

00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:23.860
or things happen in the world.

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:25.500
And then your program
computes things,

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:28.530
and they spit out
the back continually.

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:29.820
So you can group by for this.

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:31.530
You can group by
time window, so you

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:33.710
can get like a daily
summary or something.

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:36.430
Or you can also group by
and get a sliding window,

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:39.450
so you know the number of
sales in the past 10 minutes

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:42.730
or whatever, things like this.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:43.230
OK.

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:45.870
So this is the overview
of what Beam can do.

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:46.780
There is more.

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:49.710
But hopefully this gives you
a good taste and a good idea

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:51.020
of what it can do.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:54.025
And when you write
Beam, you can write it

00:21:54.025 --> 00:21:55.330
in actually multiple languages.

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:56.660
You can have your choice.

00:21:56.660 --> 00:22:00.540
There's Java, Scala,
Python, or Go.

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:01.980
And I used the Python.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:06.930
And here's what the sample
program looks like in Python.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:09.540
It's fairly straightforward.

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:13.620
The one thing to notice is that
the group by is now two lines.

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:16.320
What happens is group by
doesn't take arguments, instead

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:20.850
what you do is you write
your data as a tuple,

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:22.350
where the first
element of the tuple

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:23.790
is what you want to group by.

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:26.520
And then you just call
group by, or group by key,

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:29.610
and it's going to group
by the first element.

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:32.280
So you write these two
lines to do your group by.

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:36.180
And you can see here the
file names start with gs.

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:41.130
That's a path for storage
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:43.650
So I could say a
lot more about Beam.

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:45.990
I just want to focus on
my favorite features.

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:49.485
And so the first one
here is that Beam

00:22:49.485 --> 00:22:50.970
is a little bit
unique in the sense

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:52.930
that, when you write
a Beam program,

00:22:52.930 --> 00:22:55.760
it can actually-- it will
actually kind of cross

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.640
compile it to different runners,
to different other distributed

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:01.240
systems.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:03.450
So the one on the
left is not labeled.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:04.950
But this is Google
Cloud Dataflow,

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:08.470
so you would use this
to run it on the Cloud.

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:11.850
You can also run it on
Apache Flink, or Spark,

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:13.180
or a number of other ones.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:14.680
So this is great,
because this means

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:17.070
that you write the
same program, and you

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:19.370
could write-- you could run
it on your local cluster,

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:20.834
say using Spark.

00:23:20.834 --> 00:23:22.500
Or you could run it
on a Google cluster,

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:24.570
using Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:26.940
Or you could run it on
somebody else's cluster,

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:28.770
using one of these
other systems.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:30.704
And you know, if
one of these systems

00:23:30.704 --> 00:23:33.120
developed some great innovation
and it becomes super fast,

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:36.220
you can just choose
to use this one.

00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:37.181
So that's really nice.

00:23:37.181 --> 00:23:38.680
The other thing is
the auto-scaling,

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:41.030
I already hinted at before.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:44.390
So this graph shows this
kind of in a graphical way.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:46.760
This is what you actually
see when you're running.

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:48.800
Here it was just a toy program.

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:51.980
But the idea is that it will add
and remove computers as needed.

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:53.690
So there are two lines here.

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:55.430
So you may be curious,
the green line

00:23:55.430 --> 00:23:56.870
represents the
number of computers

00:23:56.870 --> 00:23:59.300
that the system
decided was optimal.

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:02.600
And the blue line is the number
of computers that were actually

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:03.600
running at the time.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:05.150
So you can see the blue
line just kind of quickly

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:06.550
matches up to the green line.

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:07.966
It just takes a
little bit of time

00:24:07.966 --> 00:24:09.560
to start and stop the computers.

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:11.810
And you can see this particular
computation, you know,

00:24:11.810 --> 00:24:12.990
it has multiple phases.

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:14.840
There is some sort
of, perhaps, setup

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:17.510
at the beginning, where you have
a small number of computers.

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:18.980
And then the
computation ramps up

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:21.500
and the system adds
machines as needed.

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.242
And it looks like there is a
third phase at the end, where

00:24:24.242 --> 00:24:26.450
we're doing something else
that doesn't need quite as

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:27.080
many machines.

00:24:27.080 --> 00:24:30.576
And so they quickly cut down
the number of computers.

00:24:30.576 --> 00:24:31.700
This is done automatically.

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:34.340
All you have to do is
enable auto-scaling

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:37.820
and then you can just
see what happens.

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:40.190
The third thing I wanted to
highlight about Apache Beam

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:42.902
is the many connection options.

00:24:42.902 --> 00:24:44.360
So I already
mentioned that you can

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:46.610
read from a file
on the Google Cloud

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:48.200
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:51.140
You can also read from
other people's clouds.

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:53.960
You can read from a database.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:57.006
You can read events from a
published subscribe system.

00:24:57.006 --> 00:24:59.630
You can even-- this would be for
the streaming case I mentioned

00:24:59.630 --> 00:25:00.510
before--

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:04.070
you can even connect it to
another Dataflow language

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:06.020
that's going to be
streaming events at you.

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:11.062
So you can imagine running
Apache Kafka to get some events

00:25:11.062 --> 00:25:12.270
streaming through the system.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:13.644
And you, perhaps
for some reason,

00:25:13.644 --> 00:25:14.811
already wrote this in Kafka.

00:25:14.811 --> 00:25:16.727
Then you want to do some
additional processing

00:25:16.727 --> 00:25:17.480
in Apache Beam.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:19.938
Well, you can connect the two,
and the events flow through,

00:25:19.938 --> 00:25:23.830
and now you can do your
streaming computation.

00:25:23.830 --> 00:25:29.110
And when you're running Beam,
if you're running with Dataflow,

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:30.874
then this is what it looks like.

00:25:30.874 --> 00:25:32.290
I know that the
picture is perhaps

00:25:32.290 --> 00:25:33.498
too small to see the details.

00:25:33.498 --> 00:25:35.770
But I just wanted to give
you an overview anyways.

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:37.540
So the biggest part
here on the left

00:25:37.540 --> 00:25:40.476
is a graphical representation
of your program.

00:25:40.476 --> 00:25:42.850
That's the one you gave in
when you type in your program,

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:44.650
Beam programs are graphs.

00:25:44.650 --> 00:25:47.620
And each box is labeled with
the name that you gave it.

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:49.060
These are your functions.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:50.950
And it gives you some
information about

00:25:50.950 --> 00:25:54.280
whether it's running or
not and how fast and so on.

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:56.560
And on the right, you have
some overall statistics

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:57.902
about your job.

00:25:57.902 --> 00:25:59.860
It tells you how many
computers are running it,

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:01.210
so that's the worker count.

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:03.825
It tells you how many resources
have been consumed so far.

00:26:03.825 --> 00:26:05.950
Some options that you
specified on the command line

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:06.822
are repeated here.

00:26:06.822 --> 00:26:08.530
And then, you have
the auto-scaling graph

00:26:08.530 --> 00:26:10.430
in the middle that I
already explained before.

00:26:10.430 --> 00:26:11.846
So as your computation
is running,

00:26:11.846 --> 00:26:14.150
you can see how many
computers are assigned to it.

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:16.300
And you choose to use
auto-scaling scaling, or not.

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:18.466
If you don't, it's just
going to be a straight line.

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:25.530
So that's our quick
introduction to Apache Beam.

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:28.980
And hopefully, I made you want
to see more and learn more.

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:30.780
And now we'll go
through this example

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:33.390
of marking the duplicates
using Apache Beam,

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.560
so you can see what it
looks like in action.

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.740
So just a quick
reminder, right, we

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:39.540
have this set of read records.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.170
And we want to mark--

00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:43.800
we want to update the
is duplicate fields.

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:46.380
And the rule is that
if they are unpaired,

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:48.720
and in the same position,
we mark them as duplicate.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:50.700
If they're paired and both
are in the same position,

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:52.241
and if they're a
mix, then if they're

00:26:52.241 --> 00:26:55.650
any of the position matches,
we mark it as duplicate.

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:56.790
That's the rules.

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:57.780
Let's go.

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:01.570
So here's the first
Beam program to do this.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:04.219
So our input on the left
is the set of read records.

00:27:04.219 --> 00:27:05.760
And the first thing
we're going to do

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:07.920
is check whether
they're paired or not.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:09.870
We have the paired
flag, then we're

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:12.010
going to send it to
the different branch.

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:13.560
So let's start with
the blue branch

00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:16.140
at the bottom, where the case
where they're not paired.

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:17.515
So when the reads
are not paired,

00:27:17.515 --> 00:27:20.760
we're going to use group by to
put together all the reads that

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:22.110
start at the same position.

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:24.190
So we're grouping by position.

00:27:24.190 --> 00:27:26.250
And then we look at
each group and we look

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:27.960
at how many elements there are.

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:30.270
If there is more than
one, we found duplicates,

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:31.590
so we mark them.

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:33.960
And then we're done, goes
to the storage on the end,

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:36.900
so our output set of
read records again.

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:38.770
In the case where
they're paired,

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:40.500
we need to do a
little bit more work.

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:43.049
The first thing we do
is we group them by name

00:27:43.049 --> 00:27:44.340
to put the pairs back together.

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:46.090
Remember, if two
reads are paired,

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:47.090
they have the same name.

00:27:47.090 --> 00:27:49.650
So doing this we
get the pairs back.

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:51.240
Then once we have
the pairs, we group

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:54.120
the pairs by their position
tuple, so the position

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:55.200
of each half.

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:57.220
And if you have more than
one in the same group,

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:58.845
that means that both
of their positions

00:27:58.845 --> 00:28:00.780
match, so they're duplicates.

00:28:00.780 --> 00:28:03.280
So we mark them and we're done.

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:05.725
So this is, hopefully,
fairly reasonable.

00:28:05.725 --> 00:28:08.100
I'll go through an example to
make sure that we're really

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:09.780
on the same page.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:12.610
We'll start with the
unpaired read example.

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:14.490
So here we have three reads.

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:16.890
There is blue, green,
and the dark blue.

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:20.820
And the blue and dark blue
are both at position 100,

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.380
and the green one is at
a different position.

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:23.970
So we checked.

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:27.390
Yes, they are not paired,
so we go in the blue path.

00:28:27.390 --> 00:28:29.844
And the first thing we do is
we group them by position.

00:28:29.844 --> 00:28:31.510
So we end up with
these two groups here.

00:28:31.510 --> 00:28:35.370
There's a group at position 100
and a group at position 400.

00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:37.860
You can see that the first
group has more than one element.

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:39.540
These reads are duplicates.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:42.677
So we are marking everyone,
except the first one,

00:28:42.677 --> 00:28:44.760
representing this by
circling them in the picture.

00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:48.060
This means we set
the field to true.

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:49.290
And we're done.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:52.890
Now, in the case
of paired reads,

00:28:52.890 --> 00:28:56.940
this time here I give you four
reads, two yellow, and two red.

00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:58.560
The two yellow
have the same name.

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:00.800
They're paired to the
two red, likewise.

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:03.207
And their positions
are 100 and 400.

00:29:03.207 --> 00:29:05.290
So the first thing we do
is we group them by name.

00:29:05.290 --> 00:29:08.010
So you can see now, we put
the pairs back together.

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:11.110
So you have the yellow
pair and the red pair.

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.930
And if there were read
anymore, they would be there.

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:16.882
The next thing we do is
now we group by position.

00:29:16.882 --> 00:29:19.830
So it so happens in this
example that both pairs

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:22.650
have the same positions,
they're at 100, 400.

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:24.480
So this group has
more than one element,

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:26.140
it means we found a duplicate.

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:26.880
We mark it.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:28.890
We're done.

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:30.510
So this is great.

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:33.150
And it would work,
except that there

00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:35.040
is a case we didn't go through.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:36.240
You may have noticed this.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:38.490
You may have been wondering
while I was presenting.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:41.250
What about if we have reads
that are paired mixed with

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:42.250
reads that are unpaired?

00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:46.450
There's a rule there that says
we have to mark some of them.

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:50.110
So we need to add this
for the program to work.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:51.624
So let's fix the program.

00:29:51.624 --> 00:29:53.040
So here is the
exact same program,

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:55.740
except now, I removed the
details from the boxes.

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:58.020
And I just put a
one-line description

00:29:58.020 --> 00:29:59.040
of what each box does.

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:00.700
But it's the same program.

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:02.970
So the fix is the
following, you first

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:04.630
checked if the reads
are paired or not.

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:11.250
And then, you send all the
reads down the unpaired check.

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:14.124
Because when we have
the unpaired reads,

00:30:14.124 --> 00:30:15.540
we need to mark
them as duplicates

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:18.690
if they match another unpaired
read, but also if they

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:20.700
match a paired read.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:23.031
So we are kind of merging
the red and the blue paths

00:30:23.031 --> 00:30:24.030
down here at the bottom.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:25.800
That's way I put it in purple.

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:30.090
So now we can check the unpaired
read correctly and write them

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:30.610
down.

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:32.110
And the top part
remains, of course.

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:33.526
So the paired
reads, we still need

00:30:33.526 --> 00:30:36.180
to put the pairs together and do
the processing and everything.

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:37.304
So this is the correct fix.

00:30:37.304 --> 00:30:38.850
This would work.

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:40.710
I've run this.

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:43.260
But now, you may be
wondering, well, you know,

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:46.760
these paired reads are
going through both paths.

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:50.130
It looks like we're doing
some redundant computation.

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:51.900
Could we optimize
this a little bit?

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:55.080
You know, could we
change the program, such

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:59.640
that instead of sending all
the paired reads to check,

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:02.970
we perhaps eliminate some
paired reads that can't possibly

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.970
be duplicates in this
first purple step,

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:08.070
and then only send the
remainder to the red step,

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:10.264
instead of sending them all?

00:31:10.264 --> 00:31:12.180
And, of course, the
reason I'm suggesting this

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.260
is that, yes, you can do it.

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:16.440
The key insight
here is, what can we

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:19.194
do when we only have
half of the pair?

00:31:19.194 --> 00:31:20.610
So if they're
unpaired, of course,

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:21.270
we can do the whole thing.

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:22.980
In the example
here, right, I have

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:25.431
the green and the blue read.

00:31:25.431 --> 00:31:27.930
I'm aligning them vertically
to represent the fact that they

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:29.130
have the same position.

00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:31.050
These are duplicates.

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:34.200
If you have paired reads,
here, for example, the yellow

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:36.120
and orange, I put
the dotted line

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:39.090
to show that this is
half of a paired read.

00:31:39.090 --> 00:31:42.240
We can see that these two
are the same position.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:43.079
They're aligned.

00:31:43.079 --> 00:31:45.620
But we don't know what happens
to the other half of the pair.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:47.620
So we actually don't know
whether they're duplicates

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:48.120
or not.

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:49.620
They could be duplicates.

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:52.470
Or they could be non-duplicates.

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:56.370
At this point, we
really can't tell.

00:31:56.370 --> 00:32:01.890
The orange read at
the bottom, instead,

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:04.560
this one we know it can't
possibly be a duplicate.

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:08.370
Because remember, the rule is
that both halves have to match,

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:09.090
right?

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:12.240
And at this position,
there is only one read.

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:14.339
So we know that, because
this half doesn't match,

00:32:14.339 --> 00:32:16.380
it doesn't matter what
happens to the other half.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:19.150
This particular read
cannot be a duplicate.

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:20.350
So we can eliminate it.

00:32:20.350 --> 00:32:21.450
So that's the insight.

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:24.210
We only need to take the reads
that are in this middle case

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:26.070
and send them for
further processing

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:27.870
to double check if
they're paired or--

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:30.180
sorry, if they're
duplicates or not.

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:32.510
The rest, we're done.

00:32:32.510 --> 00:32:37.090
And so just quickly here is
what the program looks like.

00:32:37.090 --> 00:32:38.970
I know the slide
looks busy, but this

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:41.940
is very similar to the
program we've seen before,

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:43.500
worked through together before.

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:47.220
It's just vertical
because I had to fit it.

00:32:47.220 --> 00:32:48.450
We start at the top left.

00:32:48.450 --> 00:32:49.770
The reads come in.

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:51.840
We group them by position.

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:54.570
And once we have
this, we can correctly

00:32:54.570 --> 00:32:57.720
mark all the unpaired
reads that are duplicates.

00:32:57.720 --> 00:32:59.430
And then, what about
the paired reads?

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:00.360
Well, we look.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:03.990
Is there more than one at
this particular location?

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:06.930
If there is more than
one, then we go, yes.

00:33:06.930 --> 00:33:09.880
And the paired reads go to
the red path on the right.

00:33:09.880 --> 00:33:11.970
So because these are
the ambiguous case.

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:14.490
If there's more than one,
they could be duplicate.

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:15.630
We don't know.

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:22.500
If there is one or fewer, than
everything goes to the output.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:24.120
We're done with those.

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:25.920
And the right path is
the same as before.

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:27.157
You put the pairs together.

00:33:27.157 --> 00:33:28.240
You check their positions.

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:30.156
If there's more than one
of the same position,

00:33:30.156 --> 00:33:31.520
you mark them as duplicates.

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:33.050
We've seen this.

00:33:33.050 --> 00:33:35.300
It works.

00:33:35.300 --> 00:33:35.800
All right.

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:38.410
So just to make things
a little bit concrete,

00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:40.690
here's what it looks
like in Python.

00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:43.846
This is actual code taken
straight from my editor.

00:33:43.846 --> 00:33:44.970
It's incomplete, of course.

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:47.380
The full program wouldn't fit.

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:49.020
But this is just
to give you a feel.

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:52.120
So the text that's bolded
is actually comments.

00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:55.450
It's the name of the
stages of the boxes.

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:59.320
And so this is what the names
that will show up in the GUI.

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:02.540
And then, I call the code.

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:04.330
They're all in the
Beam namespace.

00:34:04.330 --> 00:34:07.340
And you can see the way we do.

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:09.310
So here there's a
function I'm calling

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:11.020
keep only if has mapped mate.

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:14.050
So I only keep the paired reads.

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:17.710
And then, the step one
will put a key in it

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:21.159
that represents the name.

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:22.973
And so I'm grouping by name.

00:34:22.973 --> 00:34:25.389
And then, I'm putting a key
that represents the positions.

00:34:25.389 --> 00:34:26.805
And then I'm
grouping by position.

00:34:26.805 --> 00:34:27.770
This is step four.

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:29.860
This is exactly as we
describe in the box.

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:32.560
Then, we mark the
duplicates, and we're done.

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:36.844
So, of course, we'll need
to fill in the functions.

00:34:36.844 --> 00:34:38.510
There are plenty of
examples on the web.

00:34:38.510 --> 00:34:40.676
But this is what this
particular program looks like.

00:34:40.676 --> 00:34:42.670
And it gives you a flavor.

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:44.770
So there's one more
thing I did that I need

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:47.110
to mention for completeness.

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:49.179
By default, when you
run this on the cloud,

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:51.370
this is for Dataflow
flow in particular,

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:55.090
it will assign each worker
a 250 gigabytes disk.

00:34:55.090 --> 00:34:57.560
Turns out for this work this
was more than necessary,

00:34:57.560 --> 00:34:59.690
so I shrunk it to 85 disk.

00:34:59.690 --> 00:35:02.410
So now we need less storage,
and so it's going to be cheaper.

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:09.350
And here are the results
of the optimization step.

00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:12.864
So what I'm comparing here
is the original fixed program

00:35:12.864 --> 00:35:14.280
that we've seen
in the talk that's

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:17.830
still running Beam, and then
the optimized Beam program.

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:19.900
So this is Apache
Beam on both sides.

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:22.400
This is just the impact
of these two optimizations

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:25.400
we did, first,
shrinking the disk size,

00:35:25.400 --> 00:35:27.830
and second, the
algorithmic optimization

00:35:27.830 --> 00:35:29.190
I walked you through.

00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:31.820
And so what we can
see is that we're

00:35:31.820 --> 00:35:36.141
43% cheaper, so almost half the
costs with these small changes.

00:35:36.141 --> 00:35:37.640
A third of that
comes from shrinking

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:41.030
the disk, and another
2/3 from the optimization

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:43.100
that we walked through.

00:35:43.100 --> 00:35:45.740
And the other thing
that I wanted to do

00:35:45.740 --> 00:35:49.070
is compare this program
against our original program

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:52.050
that was running
on a single server.

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:54.680
So this single server
program was running off

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:57.800
the shelf software using
genomic libraries and stuff.

00:35:57.800 --> 00:36:00.830
And I must emphasize it
wasn't particularly tuned

00:36:00.830 --> 00:36:03.390
for performance.

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:06.502
And I'm comparing it to our
optimized Apache Beam program.

00:36:06.502 --> 00:36:08.460
And of course, this is
our own versions, right.

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:10.376
I'm not making any promise
that the same thing

00:36:10.376 --> 00:36:12.720
will happen to you if you
port your programs to Beam.

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:15.370
This is just what
happened to us.

00:36:15.370 --> 00:36:18.460
And what happened to us is
we're five times faster,

00:36:18.460 --> 00:36:21.190
5.6 times faster.

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:24.850
The cost also went up,
but it went up only 2x.

00:36:24.850 --> 00:36:27.400
So I have this graphically
here on the slide.

00:36:27.400 --> 00:36:31.480
The green bars show the
time, server on the left,

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:32.540
Apache Beam on the right.

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:36.130
So you can see the
time just crashed down.

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:38.050
And the cost on
the right in blue.

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:40.300
So again, server on the
left, the Beam version

00:36:40.300 --> 00:36:43.540
is on the right, the
cost roughly doubled.

00:36:43.540 --> 00:36:47.200
But the absolute numbers
are not that high.

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:49.600
So what I-- you know,
what's not in these--

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:51.880
we like to focus on
the numbers, right.

00:36:51.880 --> 00:36:53.800
But another thing
that's really important,

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:56.710
perhaps even more important,
is that the program now

00:36:56.710 --> 00:36:58.210
is a lot simpler.

00:36:58.210 --> 00:37:00.100
Because this program
now just need

00:37:00.100 --> 00:37:02.000
to focus on the business logic.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:05.260
In our case, that is
marking the duplicates.

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:08.080
All the parallelization
and the distribution,

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:10.150
and the fault tolerance,
and all that stuff

00:37:10.150 --> 00:37:12.160
is done by Beam and Dataflow.

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:14.020
We don't have to worry about it.

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:17.610
So this is something
that I really appreciate.

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:20.230
And then we have this other
side bonus, which is we

00:37:20.230 --> 00:37:22.420
have now a trade-off
between speed and cost.

00:37:22.420 --> 00:37:25.370
Because we can choose to
set the number of workers,

00:37:25.370 --> 00:37:27.620
the number of computers,
that are running our program.

00:37:27.620 --> 00:37:30.460
So these numbers I give
you are for 90 workers.

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:32.860
But you can also run it for
different numbers of workers.

00:37:32.860 --> 00:37:36.160
So what I plotted here
is, so each dot represents

00:37:36.160 --> 00:37:39.790
the average of five experiments
with a set number of computers.

00:37:39.790 --> 00:37:43.115
So I went from 15
to 120 workers.

00:37:43.115 --> 00:37:45.490
And I measured-- for each of
these I measured how much it

00:37:45.490 --> 00:37:48.140
costs and how long it took.

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:49.570
So the x-axis is the cost.

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:51.470
That's kind of how
much you put in.

00:37:51.470 --> 00:37:55.090
And the y-axis is the
runtime, so world clock,

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:57.880
so just measured overall.

00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:02.130
So you want to be
kind of left and low.

00:38:02.130 --> 00:38:04.560
And what you can see,
and this is actually

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:08.310
a very common pattern, is that
initially, as you add workers,

00:38:08.310 --> 00:38:10.060
your performance
improves dramatically.

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:12.137
And your cost only
increases a little bit.

00:38:12.137 --> 00:38:14.220
And that's because there
are parts of your program

00:38:14.220 --> 00:38:16.120
that parallelize really well.

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:18.690
And so, as you double
the number of workers,

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:20.800
these parts take
only half the time.

00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:23.280
And so now your program
is a lot faster.

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:24.990
In an extreme case,
you could imagine

00:38:24.990 --> 00:38:28.410
that perhaps all of your
computation is like that.

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:30.660
And if that were
the case, then when

00:38:30.660 --> 00:38:32.490
you double the
number of machines,

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:35.580
your running time
would completely halve.

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:37.530
And because you're
billed by the second,

00:38:37.530 --> 00:38:39.850
your cost would be the same.

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:42.180
So you'd be putting
twice as many computers,

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:44.470
be going twice as fast,
for the same price.

00:38:44.470 --> 00:38:46.080
So this is the ideal case.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:47.580
Here, of course,
in reality, there's

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:49.240
a little bit of a slope.

00:38:49.240 --> 00:38:51.900
And what you see is, as you
push this forward further,

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:54.300
as you put more and
more workers, now

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:56.610
the parts that parallelize
well are already

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:58.150
shrunk to a small
amount of time.

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:01.326
So they don't represent a large
part of the computation time.

00:39:01.326 --> 00:39:02.700
And the remaining
parts, perhaps,

00:39:02.700 --> 00:39:04.180
don't parallelize as well.

00:39:04.180 --> 00:39:07.380
And so they're going to
be relatively larger part

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:08.160
of the time.

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:09.690
And so the curve flattens up.

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:10.800
That's what you see.

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:12.510
It starts out really steep.

00:39:12.510 --> 00:39:13.879
And then it flattens.

00:39:13.879 --> 00:39:15.420
At which point is
best for you really

00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:19.050
depends on how much you
value time versus money.

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:22.200
This is a trade-off
that only you can make.

00:39:22.200 --> 00:39:27.210
Another way of representing the
exact same data is as a ratio.

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:30.120
So you can look at how much
more expensive than the thing

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:33.100
we started from and
how much faster.

00:39:33.100 --> 00:39:36.150
So here I put in red
the original program.

00:39:36.150 --> 00:39:38.710
I started with the blue
dots are the same as before.

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:41.550
So every dot represents the
average of five experiments

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:43.450
for a given number of workers.

00:39:43.450 --> 00:39:46.110
And so the dotted
line represents

00:39:46.110 --> 00:39:50.850
kind of what you might
expect if you were taking

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:53.100
the original program
and just if you

00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:55.620
were able to run
it on two machines

00:39:55.620 --> 00:39:57.160
and get it twice as fast.

00:39:57.160 --> 00:40:00.545
So the dotted line represents
this linear speed up of,

00:40:00.545 --> 00:40:01.920
you know, if you
double the cost,

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.440
perhaps you'll get
twice the speed.

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:07.189
But, of course, in our case,
this is not the same program.

00:40:07.189 --> 00:40:08.730
This is a completely
new program that

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:11.220
is written cloud-native
in Beam and has

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:13.470
different characteristics.

00:40:13.470 --> 00:40:17.700
And so what we see
is that it shifts

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:18.870
kind of up and to the right.

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:21.040
It's more expensive
but much faster.

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:23.560
And as we add to the number
of workers, it goes up.

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:27.670
If I were to add more, I
expect this would flatten out.

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:31.480
I just didn't run the
experiments with more.

00:40:31.480 --> 00:40:35.350
So that's what it looks like.

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:38.332
Just a few takeaways,
so, you know,

00:40:38.332 --> 00:40:40.540
if you can remember just
one thing about Apache Beam,

00:40:40.540 --> 00:40:45.820
is think of it as it's an
abstraction about how the data

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:47.650
flows through your computation.

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:49.480
And putting your
computation in these terms

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:52.300
allows it to get
really high degrees

00:40:52.300 --> 00:40:54.980
of parallelism and performance.

00:40:54.980 --> 00:40:59.260
This is really good when you're
computing with lots of data

00:40:59.260 --> 00:41:01.370
items that are the same type.

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:03.160
This is a common
pattern in life science.

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:05.270
You may also see
it in your field.

00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:07.870
The most common
operations are group by

00:41:07.870 --> 00:41:09.430
and map or parallel do.

00:41:09.430 --> 00:41:11.170
And so group by
will put together

00:41:11.170 --> 00:41:13.714
elements that match in
some way that you specify.

00:41:13.714 --> 00:41:15.130
And the other one
is when you want

00:41:15.130 --> 00:41:17.710
to do an operation on
every element in a group,

00:41:17.710 --> 00:41:18.980
or in the collection.

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:21.430
There is more. as you've seen.

00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:24.460
Apache Beam is great when
you're writing a new program.

00:41:24.460 --> 00:41:27.880
You can write your program
in this cloud-native way.

00:41:27.880 --> 00:41:31.330
If instead you already have
a large amount of software

00:41:31.330 --> 00:41:35.419
that you can't modify, you
want to run as is on the cloud,

00:41:35.419 --> 00:41:37.210
perhaps you want to
use something else that

00:41:37.210 --> 00:41:39.760
lets you just rent a computer
and run things there,

00:41:39.760 --> 00:41:43.740
like Kubernetes, or our
very own dsub that's

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:44.800
an open source tool.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:46.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

